// Justification vocabulary — the chain-resident half of `docs/design/judgements-and-warrants.tex`.
//
// The paper separates two strata and forbids any rule connecting them:
//
//   Judgement(L, t, T)      a checker of logic L verified `t : T`        FACTIVE
//   JustifiedBy(j, P)       `j` grounds a claim to P                     NOT factive
//
// This file declares the second, plus the two chain-resident classes that carry them: a
// `justification:Declaration` asserts a proposition a grounds term may cite, and a
// `justification:Conclusion` pairs a proposition with the grounds justifying it, as one
// checked judgement. `eigentt:Judgement` — the first stratum — is declared in core.
//
// The composition is one-directional, each layer a statement about the one below (paper §4):
//
//   Judgement(kernel, c, Grounds(P))   a checker verified the grounds term c
//   Grounds(P)                         the grounds for P
//   P                                    the proposition
//
// Nothing reduces the top to `Judgement(kernel, t, P)`. The types differ and no rule relates
// them, which is what makes substituting grounds for a proof inexpressible rather than merely
// discouraged (paper §2). The `Verified` ground is the configuration with no middle layer: the
// chain holds `Judgement(L, t, P)` directly.
//
// THREE GROUNDS (paper §6):
//
//   Verified   establishes `t : P`               no bridging premise — the same proposition
//   Observed   establishes a recording occurred  premise supplied by the consumer
//   Declared   establishes that agent a asserted P   premise is trust in a
//
// `Computed` and `Sampled` are not grounds. They are readings of a term's shape: `Computed` is
// `app(declared(f : I -> O), observed(input))`, `Sampled` a bare `observed` leaf. Neither is
// stored as a state name, and neither yields a `Verified` ground — in `Computed` the typing
// `f : I -> O` is itself declared. A dependent typing `f : (i : I) -> O(i)`, proven rather than
// asserted, with the input committed as a term, makes `f i : O(i)` checkable by the kernel and
// does establish `Verified` for that input.
//
// WHAT IS TRUSTED. `Verified` is provable: a committed judgement discharges the witness through
// a constructor. `Declared` and `Observed` are postulated — the kernel asserts them as proof
// constants under a constant specification, which is the correct operation for an attribution,
// since no verification of `an agent claimed this` or `a recording occurred` is possible. That
// constant specification is a TCB element alongside the native type checker, each hosted external
// checker, and each formal comorphism (paper §8).
//
// IRIs match the kernel's `well_known` constants (`CHAIN_WITNESS_IS_*_AS`, etc.) — do not rename
// without updating `kernel/src/ontology/well_known.rs` in lockstep.

namespace core          = "urn:eigenius:core";
namespace eigentt       = "urn:eigenius:eigentt";
namespace witness       = "urn:eigenius:witness";
namespace justification = "urn:eigenius:justification";
namespace prov       = "urn:eigenius:prov";

// ── ChainWitness predicates ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `witness:IsDeclaredAs`, `witness:IsObservedAs` and `witness:IsVerifiedAs` are declared in CORE
// (`ontologies/core/core-ontology.json`), not here, because the kernel constructs their
// inhabitants: `layer::synthesize_chain_witness` returns a `Val::ChainWitness` when the layer's
// witness index holds a matching key, and a type the kernel inhabits cannot be owned by a layer
// above it. They are zero-constructor inductives in `Prop`, opaque to ESL — no surface
// constructor exposes an inhabitant. The grounding constructors below reference them across
// the layer boundary, which is the ordinary direction.

// ── justification:Grounds ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The paper writes the justification stratum `JustifiedBy(j, P)`, naming the relation between a
// term and a proposition. Here the grounds ARE the term: an inhabitant of `Grounds(P)` is
// a constructor tree over the algebra, so the family is indexed by the proposition alone and the
// derivation is the value. `support` walks that value; two derivations of one P are distinct
// values.
//
// `Grounds : Prop -> Type 2`, inhabiting a Type rather than `Prop` so that a grounds term is
// stored and re-checkable.
//
// **Why `Type 2`.** A constructor argument's sort may not exceed the inductive's own, or a large
// type is smuggled into a small one and Girard's paradox follows. `instantiate` binds `T : Type 1`,
// and `Type 1` is `Sort 2`, so that argument sits at `Sort 3`; the inductive must be at `Sort 3`
// or above, which is `Type 2`. The constraint is enforced by nanoda's `check_ctor` universe check
// (`references/nanoda_lib/src/inductive.rs:904`). This costs nothing at use sites: a
// grounds term's TYPE sits a universe higher, its VALUES are unchanged, and nothing checks
// `justification:Grounds(p)` against a fixed sort.
//
// **No implication introduction.** No constructor produces `Grounds(A -> B)`: `app` yields
// `B`, `sum_*` yield `P`, `instantiate` yields `P(x)`. An implication therefore enters only through
// a grounding — asserted as a resource, witnessed by a trace. There is no deduction theorem, so a
// bridge between vocabularies cannot be derived; it must be Declared. Quantifying that bridge and
// eliminating with `instantiate` is what lets one bridge serve many claims.

data justification:Grounds : Prop -> Type 2 {
    description = "The grounds for P, retained whole. An inhabitant of Grounds(P) IS the justification term: a constructor tree with three categorical groundings at its leaves and composition operators above them, indexed by the proposition it grounds. It does not assert P — a grounds term records grounds, and no rule turns Grounds(P) into P, which is the non-factivity the two-stratum separation requires. Seven constructors: `declared`, `observed` and `verified`, each consuming a witness:Is*As that the kernel synthesizes from the layer's witness index rather than from anything an author writes; `app` for Artemov Application; `sum_l` and `sum_r`, which require BOTH branches to be justified and differ only in which constructor was chosen, that choice being the record of the preferred branch; and `instantiate`, which narrows a universal proposition to an instance and adds no ground, since eliminating a quantifier introduces none. It inhabits Type 2 rather than Prop so a grounds term is stored and re-checkable, and Type 2 rather than Type 0 because instantiate binds T : Type 1 and a constructor argument's sort may not exceed the inductive's own.";

    declared :
        forall (iri : core:iri, P : Prop) =>
        witness:IsDeclaredAs(iri, P) ->
        justification:Grounds(P),

    observed :
        forall (iri : core:iri, P : Prop) =>
        witness:IsObservedAs(iri, P) ->
        justification:Grounds(P),

    verified :
        forall (iri : core:iri, P : Prop) =>
        witness:IsVerifiedAs(iri, P) ->
        justification:Grounds(P),

    // Artemov Application: a justification of `A -> B` applied to one of `A` justifies `B`.
    //
    // Both propositions are implicit. `B` is the term's own index, so the type the
    // application is checked against fixes it. `A` occurs in no index at all — it is determined
    // by inferring the first argument and reading the domain of its `A -> B`. Writing either
    // would restate what the arguments already say.
    app implicit(A, B) :
        forall (A : Prop, B : Prop) =>
        justification:Grounds(A -> B) ->
        justification:Grounds(A) ->
        justification:Grounds(B),

    // Artemov Sum. Both branches must be justified, which departs from LP's axiom: `support`
    // reads Sum disjunctively, so an unchecked branch would answer a counterfactual with a
    // ground nobody established. `sum_l` and `sum_r` share a type; the constructor chosen is
    // what records which branch was preferred.
    sum_l implicit(P) :
        forall (P : Prop) =>
        justification:Grounds(P) ->
        justification:Grounds(P) ->
        justification:Grounds(P),

    sum_r implicit(P) :
        forall (P : Prop) =>
        justification:Grounds(P) ->
        justification:Grounds(P) ->
        justification:Grounds(P),

    // Narrow a universal to an instance. It carries its premise's grounds through unchanged,
    // which is what "specialisation adds no ground" means.
    //
    // Nothing here is implicit, `T` included. `T` reaches the index only inside `P(x)`, and `P`
    // applied to a bound variable is a higher-order pattern — outside the first-order fragment
    // `unify` implements (D48 §3.1). Solving it from the premise argument fails for the same
    // reason: the domain of `forall (y : T) => P(y)` would fix `T`, but the codomain is `P(y)`
    // and unification of the whole argument type has to succeed for any of it to count.
    // Widening that fragment is a decision about EigenTT, tracked in D88 §6.
    instantiate :
        forall (T : Type 1, P : T -> Prop, x : T) =>
        justification:Grounds(forall (y : T) => P(y)) ->
        justification:Grounds(P(x)),
}

// ── justification:Declaration ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A chain-resident resource carrying a proposition: a literature rule, a statistical-to-domain
// bridge, a claim that an analysis plan denotes a function of its input, an instrument reading
// put on chain.
//
// **Warrant applies to exactly these, and the test is mechanical** (paper §5). Warrant asks what
// evidence exists for a proposition, so a resource carrying none cannot be asked the question —
// asking it is a category error rather than an unanswered question. Requiring the proposition is
// where that test is enforced.
//
// **Ground-neutral.** `Grounds.declared(iri, P)` and `Grounds.observed(iri, P)` both cite
// a resource of this kind; which ground applies is which provenance trace the resource carries,
// not what class it is. Provenance and warrant are orthogonal axes, so a resource's origin does
// not fix its evidential standing. `enc:EncodedClaim` is the parser-produced special case.
//
// The witness a citation consumes is minted from the resource's trace, and `IsDeclaredAs(iri, P)`
// cannot exist for a resource that states no `P`. Without the requirement below, that failure
// surfaces as a missing witness at grounds-check time rather than as a missing property at
// commit — later, and further from the cause.

class justification:Declaration {
    description = "An assertion, by an accountable agent, that a proposition holds — a literature rule, a statistical-to-domain bridge, a claim that a plan denotes a function of its input. Cited as a ground by justification:Grounds.declared. REQUIRES BOTH halves: the proposition, without which the resource is not citable and warrant is not a question that applies to it; and the attribution, without which it is not a declaration at all. Requiring only the first is what the predecessor class did, and the first is the half every proposition-bearing resource has anyway. NOT a container for observations: an observed ground cites the measurement resource itself — a SampleSet, an ingested file — which carries its own domain structure and needs no class here. Its proposition is its own eigentt:proposition or the D39 §4.1 `core:Asserts(iri)` default.";
    requires eigentt:proposition, prov:was_attributed_to;
    recommends prov:had_primary_source, core:description, core:short_name;
}

// ── justification:Conclusion ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The chain-resident pairing of a proposition with the grounds that justify it, carried as
// one judgement so that the pairing is the thing checked.
//
// It subclasses nothing. A conclusion is produced by checking rather than measured or asserted,
// but that is a fact about how it came to exist — provenance, recorded by its trace — and
// classifying by origin is what the two-axis separation removes.
//
// A synthesis that builds on an earlier conclusion composes with `Grounds.app`, taking the
// cited conclusion's CERTIFICATE as its antecedent. There is no citation of a conclusion by IRI
// as a ground in its own right: the three grounds are the only leaves.

class justification:Conclusion {
    description = "A chain-resident reasoning step: the thing a justification concludes. Carries ONE required judgement — `holds(kernel, c, Grounds(P))`, read as `the kernel verified that the grounds c ground a claim to P`. It does NOT assert P: a grounds term records grounds, and no rule turns Grounds(P) into P. The proposition and the grounds both sit inside that judgement's type, so one check establishes that these grounds ground this proposition, rather than several checks with nothing requiring them to concern the same claim. Named Conclusion because Claim is taken by enc:EncodedClaim.";
    requires justification:grounds_judgement;
    recommends justification:proof_judgement;
}

property justification:grounds_judgement : core:inductive {
    description = "The checked judgement this conclusion rests on: `holds(kernel, c, Grounds(P))`. The proposition P appears inside the TYPE, so checking the judgement checks that the term inhabits the Grounds type for that proposition — one obligation, discharged by the uniform check-mode rule. Also the source of the conclusion's canonical proposition for witness emission: P is projected out of the judgement's type rather than read from a separate slot.";
    class_types eigentt:Judgement;
    domain justification:Conclusion;
}

property justification:proof_judgement : core:inductive {
    description = "An optional SECOND judgement: `holds(logic, t, P)`, a proof of the conclusion's proposition in a logic the system hosts a checker for. Distinct from justification:grounds_judgement and never merged with it — that one says `c grounds P`, this one says `t proves P`, and conflating them is the substitution the two-stratum separation exists to make inexpressible. A conclusion carrying this is the Verified configuration: the chain holds a proof of P itself, not only grounds for a claim to it. Present only where a proof term exists; its absence is the normal case and carries no penalty.";
    class_types eigentt:Judgement;
    domain justification:Conclusion;
}

// `justification:subject_iri` and `justification:refutes` were declared here and are gone (D89 §3).
// Neither had a reader: `subject_iri` described itself as a first-class EigenQL index and no index
// registered it, and `refutes` had no writer either — a marker held for chain-merge work that has
// not arrived. Aboutness is recoverable from the proposition, which mentions its subject; a
// supersession marker is minted when the merge semantics that give it meaning are built.

// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Warrant: computed, never stored
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// The chain stores provenance relations, justification terms, committed judgements, and the
// declared premises those terms cite. The warrant is evaluated from the term (paper §6.2). No
// resource carries a grade.
//
// A stored grade can contradict its own evidence; a computed one cannot. A resource labelled
// Verified without a valid proof term would be a representable but invalid state, and nothing
// resolves the conflict once it exists. Computing instead makes that state unrepresentable, so
// no validation rule is needed to exclude it. It also keeps the answer current: if an accountable
// party withdraws a declaration `f : I -> O`, every dependent conclusion falls back to its
// observations with no resource edited.
//
// WHAT THE RETAINED TERM ANSWERS, and a scalar could not:
//
//   is this fully verified?              every ground of SOME alternative is Verified
//   what does it rest on unproved?       the Declared grounds
//   which agents are we trusting?        declared_by of those grounds
//   which measurements?                  the Observed grounds
//   would it survive losing X?           SOME alternative cites X nowhere
//
// The last is the argument for retaining the term at all: a scalar records what the grounds came
// to, not what they were, leaving nothing to recompute over.
//
// SUM IS DISJUNCTIVE. A conclusion resting on `sum(verified(a), declared(b))` IS fully verified —
// the `a` branch carries it alone. Reading Sum conjunctively would understate every conclusion
// written with a fallback, which is the shape a careful author writes.
//
// ONE COMPUTATION, MANY SLICES. Computing the term's support — its disjunctive normal form — is
// the whole cost; slicing that support is free. So the algebra exposes the slices directly rather
// than a projection-kind selector, and the one genuinely parameterised question, the
// counterfactual, takes the IRI it is asked about.
//
// **These are Rust-API answers.** `support`, `is_fully_verified`, `leaves_of`, `survives_without`
// and `cited_iris` in `kernel/src/justification/` are called on a retained term. EigenQL cannot
// express them and there is no chain-resident request or report shape: a justification term lands
// as `Value::Json` (`Value` has no inductive variant, `kernel/src/ontology/resource.rs`) and
// `values_equal` (`kernel/src/query/functions.rs`) has no `Json` arm, so two terms never compare
// equal and a pattern can never bind one. Patterns admit scalars and arrays; no built-in decodes
// a term. "Warrant becomes a query" must not be read as "an EigenQL query" — the λProlog
// extension over EigenQL's Datalog foundation is what would make it one, and is tracked
// separately.
//
// PROVENANCE, by contrast, IS an EigenQL query, which is the point of making it relational:
// `prov:was_attributed_to`, `prov:was_generated_by`, `prov:used` and `prov:had_primary_source`
// are resource-typed, so "which claims rest on this instrument" is a join.

// ── Well-foundedness ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Because the warrant is evaluated against the current head rather than frozen at commit, a later
// layer can change what an earlier term means. That admits a cycle: a claim `C` rests on a bare
// observation of a plan `f`; a later layer declares `f : I -> O` citing `C` as its evidence; `C`
// now reads as an application whose support graph contains the declaration whose own support
// contains `C`.
//
// The condition is that a premise's support graph may not transitively include the premise
// itself, and a violating justification is rejected at commit — a well-formedness condition on
// terms, structurally the same kind of check as positivity on an inductive declaration. It is
// enforced over the `core:mentions` index, which projects a term's internal references into the
// graph as edges, so detection is ordinary transitive-closure cycle detection over incoming
// resources (`kernel/src/justification/wellfounded.rs`).
//
// A declared premise is exempt, and vacuously so: it has no support graph to inspect, because its
// bridge rests on institutional trust rather than on a derived proposition. Artemov's constant
// specifications permit self-referential axioms `c : A(c)`, and that self-reference is necessary
// to realize certain S4 theorems in LP. Postulated self-reference is sound; derived circularity is
// not, and only derived circularity has a support graph to check.

// ── Comorphisms ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The Lean-4 verification institution is the checker that turns a committed Lean proof term into
// a `Verified` ground: re-checking a proof yields a judgement, and that judgement discharges the
// `witness:IsVerifiedAs` witness `Grounds.verified` consumes. That direction is live, and its
// comorphism is a TCB element — the risk it carries is not that the Lean kernel accepts
// falsehoods but that the translated proposition `P'` fails to denote the chain proposition `P`
// (paper §7.2).
//
// The opposite direction — translating an EigenTT term into Lean — has no declaration here. It
// requires a chain-resident `program:Component` implementing an EigenTT → Lean term/type
// translation, which D30 does not specify: D30 covers the Eigon class → Lean structure mirror, a
// different translation. Validation rule 15 (`check_comorphism_well_formedness`) rejects a stub
// authoring, so the absence is the correct state until that Component exists. gh #73 tracks it.
